WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:09.709
Malcolm Grant: Let us move on, then, to the
next item on the agenda, which is the item

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:14.860
on sustainability and transformation plans.
We are going to be joined by Jo Lenaghan.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:20.759
Jo is the director of the Five Year Forward
View Chief Executive Officers’ Board, and

00:00:20.759 --> 00:00:26.009
perhaps we will ask Jo to say a few words
about that before she introduces the paper.

00:00:26.009 --> 00:00:27.050
Jo, over to you.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.619
Jo Lenaghan: Thank you, Malcolm. Yes, a complicated
job title: it essentially means that, once

00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:38.160
a month, the six ALB chief executives come
together to oversee and drive forward delivery

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.719
of the Five Year Forward View, a vision that
we set out in 2014. And as part of that, on

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:51.739
their behalf, I lead and coordinate the STP
process that we set out in the planning guidance.

00:00:51.739 --> 00:00:56.879
I think it is worth reminding ourselves that
it was just December past that we agreed we

00:00:56.879 --> 00:01:01.979
would require from the system a sustainability
and transformation plan. So, instead of each

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:07.250
individual organisation producing an annual
plan, we asked people to come together to

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:15.750
agree a local footprint and to develop a multi-year
plan across a geographical area on behalf

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:20.369
of their populations. That is really something
quite new and radical we have never really

00:01:20.369 --> 00:01:25.009
done before, so what I wanted to do today
was to update the Board on our progress on

00:01:25.009 --> 00:01:28.390
that.
We asked each of the footprints to send us

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:35.840
in their initial submission to us in 15th
April. We received all those, and we used

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:46.859
those as a basis for a series of conversations
which ran throughout May with 24 footprints.

00:01:46.859 --> 00:01:51.789
The point of these conversations was to make
sure that we had a shared understanding of

00:01:51.789 --> 00:01:58.049
the challenges on each patch; that we could
identify the big issues that they needed to

00:01:58.049 --> 00:02:02.810
focus on to move forward and what we as a
national team could do to help; and to agree

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:08.270
what we would like to see in their 30th June
submission so we could just start taking it

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:11.870
forward.
The day consisted of a panel made up of at

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:17.080
least two ALB chief executives – so it might
be Simon and Jim on one day, or Ian Cumming

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:24.360
from HEE and Duncan Selbie on another – the
regional directors from NHSI and NHS England,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:31.200
and the Chief Executive of the Local Government
Association, Mark Lloyd, recognising the absolutely

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.500
joint way we need to work with local government.
Each footprint lead brought along the chief

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:43.260
executives of their trusts, the accounting
officers of their CCGs and, again, the leaders

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:49.510
of their local councils. Then, as I say, we
had some really helpful conversations.

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:53.940
So Simon I am sure will have observations,
as will Richard, as one of the RDs who attended

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.659
at the North. However, my own reflections
would be, without exception, everyone welcomed

00:02:58.659 --> 00:03:03.409
the conversation. Now, people would say that,
with their bosses in the room – thanks for

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:09.129
the conversation – but I think genuinely
there were some people who had clearly been

00:03:09.129 --> 00:03:14.799
working for many years on different strategies,
and I think they welcomed the STP process

00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:19.330
as something that would give them pace and
momentum; some focus for stuff that they know

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:23.599
they want to do. There were other areas that
had literally never come together before,

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:27.400
and it was the first time they were having
a conversation about what was the best thing

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.890
to do for their population. Again, they welcomed
the incentive and encouragement to do what

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:37.799
they thought was the right thing. So I think
the energy in the room was genuine.

00:03:37.799 --> 00:03:40.579
The other reflection: I think that everyone,
again, was at very different starting points.

00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:43.970
As I’ve just said, some of them it was literally
the first time they had come together, whereas

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:48.069
others were really polishing up some really
well-worked plans. I’ll come back to that,

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:55.510
because that is key to how we take it forward.
We had to keep stressing, I think, the ‘both/and’

00:03:55.510 --> 00:04:02.500
message that Simon and Jim were giving: that
this is not just about creating a lovely strategy

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:08.640
for 2021; it has to bridge back to the here
and now, the issues that Simon was talking

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.140
about, for 2016/2017. It is not difficult
to create a strategy; it is hard to connect

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:18.680
it with the actual need to do now. So the
conversations were: how do we make those two

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:24.419
connect in the real world? Workforce came
up as a key issue in practically every footprint,

00:04:24.419 --> 00:04:28.910
particularly the most challenged areas, so
we were grateful for the support HEE will

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:33.550
offer us there.
I think the last reflection I would have is

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:38.780
both the calibre of the local authority representatives
and their collaborative nature. Quite often

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:46.250
in the conversations, the energy and the means
and the goodwill to take things forward was

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:53.009
coming from local authority colleagues. I
should not have to observe that, but I think,

00:04:53.009 --> 00:04:58.330
just being honest, it was an issue, and I
think it is one that we can really harness

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:04.090
our efforts to, going forward. Mark Lloyd,
I think, has been an excellent colleague in

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:05.280
that respect.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:10.740
So, in terms of what next, we have asked for
people to make a submission for 30th June.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:15.680
You may have seen that we published guidance
last week; guidance in the sense that it was

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.970
essentially a two-page letter. We are trying
to make it really simple what it is that we

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:26.509
want from people. So we set out there that
we need each footprint to make sure they have

00:05:26.509 --> 00:05:31.160
a shared understanding of the challenge, where
they are at in terms of the three issues:

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:36.630
the money, the health and the quality on their
patch; to identify the three or five critical

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:42.160
decisions that they need to make if they are
to actually tackle those three challenges

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:48.160
within the indicative allocations we set out
for 2021; and to be able to spell out what

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:54.610
the anticipated benefits are for their public
and their populations in each year; and being

00:05:54.610 --> 00:06:00.870
clear about what actions are needed from individual
organisations, what they need to do collectively

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:07.740
and what we need to do nationally to help
them; and also to set out their plans then

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:14.440
for building consensus engagement with those
partners – patients, public, the voluntary

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:18.580
sector, clinicians, crucially – and that
should be a key part.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:23.080
Now, having said that, we do recognise that
everyone is very much in a different stage

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:26.940
of this journey, so in that guidance we did
say that how much detail you are able to go

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:33.930
into on all those things will depend upon
where you are on your journey. So we do recognise

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:40.729
some plans will be more detailed than others,
and we are considering now in terms of the

00:06:40.729 --> 00:06:48.229
July conversations that we have had with people,
how we sign off will now be a bespoke process.

00:06:48.229 --> 00:06:52.770
Rather than trying to sign everybody off on
the same day, we are considering a more pipeline

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:58.069
approach; recognising that those who are most
advanced and have a compelling case for change

00:06:58.069 --> 00:07:04.270
that we think stacks up, they are more likely
to get early approval to get on with it; those

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:08.379
who are in the middle or towards the end,
they are going to need more support, they

00:07:08.379 --> 00:07:11.569
may need to do more work. We will need to
work with them to get them over the line,

00:07:11.569 --> 00:07:17.560
so there will be phases or waves in terms
of how we take that through, weaving and building

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:21.419
that into the 2017/2018 planning process and
beyond. There is a lot of work to be done

00:07:21.419 --> 00:07:24.759
on that, but that is our current thinking.

00:07:24.759 --> 00:07:30.250
That brings me onto the support for footprints.
You will see in the paper I have listed that

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:34.090
both the national policy directors and the
regional directors are doing a lot of work

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:39.620
with the footprints to help them not just
deliver a plan, but actually to drive the

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:45.819
implementation that will take forward the
visions that people are setting out. So we

00:07:45.819 --> 00:07:51.360
have got streams of work in strand. Is it
enough just yet? No. I think we all need to

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:56.690
look at how we work individually as ALBs with
our regions and how we work collectively amongst

00:07:56.690 --> 00:08:02.039
ourselves. So, again, we are doing work amongst
the ALBs in terms of how we best support people

00:08:02.039 --> 00:08:08.909
on not just producing a plan for 30th&nbsp;June
and beyond, but how do we drive a five-year

00:08:08.909 --> 00:08:14.479
programme of change across the system that
does set out the Five Year Forward View vision

00:08:14.479 --> 00:08:17.069
that we all want to see?

00:08:17.069 --> 00:08:20.629
So that was just a very quick run-through,
Chair, in terms of the progress we have made.

00:08:20.629 --> 00:08:26.470
I just wanted the Board to be updated on that,
but also to share their observations and recommendations

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:33.000
on how we achieve the purpose and maintain
the energy and momentum I think we have actually

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:40.340
triggered in the system, and not drown ourselves
in too many processes. So that is all I wanted

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:41.120
to say.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.950
Malcolm Grant: Jo, thank you very much for
the introduction. Let me open up for discussion

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:50.400
around the table. Noel?
Noel Gordon: Jo, this is great progress, and

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:57.590
one of the areas which I think we are testing
is our ability to pool sovereignty and create

00:08:57.590 --> 00:09:07.800
what you described as a collaborative process.
As some of the STPs go first, do you think

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:13.580
there is a way in which it is possible to
transfer the learning from those who have

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:21.770
succeeded in creating consensus to those that are struggling to create consensus? In other words,

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:30.660
is there a mechanism by which consensus-building techniques can be transferred across the system,

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:36.190
or is this highly individualistic and situational?
Jo Lenaghan: Thanks, Noel. So, yes, that is

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:41.770
a key question. So we have put the 44 in touch
with each other. We have got a social media

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:47.030
platform where they can share ideas and experience.
As we went round and did the visits, we have

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:52.560
connected different people that we think may
be useful for each other, and the chief executives

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:56.770
now have a monthly telephone conference call
with all the 44 together. So we are trying

00:09:56.770 --> 00:10:04.100
to build them as a system leadership team
and, I think, share the learning. However,

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:08.710
my personal view is that a lot of that is
actually about personalities and relationships

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:14.500
and trust, and that you need to build that,
and that the STP process is a forcing mechanism.

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:18.690
It is a useful tool to actually help build
and grow that.

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:25.230
I should also add that we have commissioned
the leadership board that reports to the Five

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:29.900
Year Forward View board to develop a whole
programme of system leadership and support

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:34.070
and tools to help people to do exactly just
what you have said.

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:36.750
Noel Gordon: Thank you.
Victor Adebowale: Might I just add to that?

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:43.700
I am curious about two things, if I may. The
first is what your view is of how they think

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:48.460
about other system players, like the not-for-profit
sector, because they were not in the room

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:51.260
and they are big players.
The second is the question of evaluation,

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:56.490
because we wait to the end of a programme
and then evaluate it. I am just wondering

00:10:56.490 --> 00:11:02.350
what the evaluation programme process is going
to be? Is it a wait until the end? Are we

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:06.520
evaluating as we go along?
Jo Lenaghan: On that second one, actually

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:11.470
the King’s Fund are evaluating this process
as we go along. So they have picked up four

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:15.850
footprints, and that was my steer to the King’s
Fund. It is no point telling us afterwards

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:21.390
what we should have done; we need to learn
as we go, and I have a meeting with them next

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:25.160
week. So this is action learning, because
it is very iterative; we have never done this

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:32.190
before. So we are keen to build the learning
and, with the 44, to feedback and take each

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:35.990
point.
In terms of the not-for-profit sector, they

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:40.550
have not been in the room to date, but I think
you are absolutely right, because we are trying

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:44.750
to build a change management programme. I
think in some areas it depends where they

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:50.480
are at; where the NHS itself is not yet used
to pooling sovereignty, they have got to get

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:54.820
those relationships right. Ditto, some are
not used to working with local authorities,

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:58.790
it is very new to them. Some are much more
advanced, and have done that. So, again, they

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:05.060
will be in different places. We will encourage
and steer and show what we think good is,

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:08.570
but just be honest about it; people are in
different places on that.

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:14.420
Malcolm Grant: David and John.
David Roberts: Jo, this is, I think, absolutely

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:22.360
essential for the sustainability of the sector.
For it to be successful, it is highly likely

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:27.480
there will be some quite radical change, which
suggests to me two key questions. I think,

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:37.740
firstly, it is the capacity and capability
– your assessment of the STP areas – to

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:43.010
manage that change and to implement it. The
design is the easy bit. The implementation

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:51.020
is the hard bit, and the NHS is not full of
huge successes of big structural change that

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:57.330
this is. Then the second thing is that a key
part of that change process will be taking

00:12:57.330 --> 00:13:07.330
the local populations with us as we go on
the journeys. I wonder – my experience would

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:11.770
be to start early rather than later, doing
that. Therefore, I wonder if you could just

00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:15.910
talk a little bit about where we are up to?
So, change capacity, capability, what we are

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:19.970
doing to boost that and bolster it; and then
bringing people with us.

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:24.840
Jo Lenaghan: Yes. So I should add that it
is not my assessment; I am co‑ordinating

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:28.900
the process on behalf of ALBs and the chief
executive and, indeed, the regional directors

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:34.460
are absolutely key to this. So we do not have
a big central team doing this. I think a design

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:38.010
principle is that change will be delivered
in the regions and the localities. That is

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:44.090
where the capacity needs to be, in a sense.
I and my very small team should vanish away,

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:50.170
so we do not perpetuate that dysfunctionality.
So absolutely, that is a key part of the assessment;

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:54.610
both the assessment of the capacity and capability
within each footprint, but not with a view

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:59.170
to marking homework. Then the question is:
well, what can we do? How do we do that? I

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:03.040
know some of my national colleagues may have
already given some thought to how you might

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:08.130
distribute resource and address that problem,
both in the regions and localities. I am not

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:14.480
going to go into too much detail, but that
is top of our list. Also, not just assessing

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:18.750
it but what we actually then practically do
about it. I think the pipeline approach will

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:25.140
help us manage that, because then our job
is to proactively manage people towards being

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:30.230
able to come up with a coherent plan. We are
not passive recipients of plans; we are proactive

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:34.240
partners in supporting people to develop a
plan.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:38.380
So in terms of local populations, you are
absolutely right: that is how you deliver

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:42.980
change. I also think that is why local government
colleagues will be so keen on this, because

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:48.550
they are much closer to local populations
than we are on a daily basis. They have a

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:52.530
lot of excellent experience in doing that,
and their advice to us is exactly the same:

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:57.940
the earlier you start the conversations, the
better. I think we have a few examples around

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:02.130
the country where that has worked and where
that has been the case in the past, and we

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:07.790
can draw on those and share those with people.
We have got quite a bit programme of work

00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:14.070
behind the scenes to actually pull that together,
to enable people to deliver the changes and

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:18.570
the benefits that are necessary. However,
I absolutely agree: that will be the one thing

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:22.550
that actually makes this work well.
Victor Adebowale: Sorry, just it is worth

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:28.300
pointing out the patient/public involvement
taskforce that myself and Rachel are chairing

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:32.870
will need to be working with you on building
that knowledge and giving the Board line of

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:36.300
sight as to what is actually happening.
Malcolm Grant: John?

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:43.100
John Burn: Just extending that point of clarification:
when do these hit the public domain? Do they

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:49.420
all come out together, or will each STP issue
its report once you have signed off and agreed

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:54.280
a sort of strategy? How does that work?
Jo Lenaghan: So it comes back to language;

00:15:54.280 --> 00:16:00.790
when is a plan a plan? Until it is agreed,
it is work in progress. These are the basis

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:05.060
for conversations, so what we have advised
people is that they will not be published

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:09.580
until they actually are signed off and approved
as a plan; there is no point getting people

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:14.200
excited or interested in plans if they are
never going to come to fruition. So it is

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:18.300
a fine judgement. It will be as and when they
are agreed, locally and nationally, that,

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:22.420
‘Yes, this is what we think we want to do.
There is a compelling case for change.’

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:26.720
That is when we will share them, when it is
an agreed position. As I said, if we go down

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:31.940
the pipeline route, that will be a staggered
approach as opposed to in one day.

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:37.040
John Burn: You imply that there is an encouragement
to get it out to the local community so that

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:41.660
people can comment and contribute.
Jo Lenaghan: Absolutely. Remember, in most

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:46.440
areas they have been working on some sorts
of change in some areas, so they should be

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:50.990
drawing on existing insight, existing engagement.
They should not be starting with a blank sheet

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:56.910
of paper. We have said to people: they all
should be basing their changes on insight

00:16:56.910 --> 00:17:01.510
and engagement that they already have, and
then do more to build and take that forward

00:17:01.510 --> 00:17:06.929
with people. This should be a dynamic process,
not just a static, sending in a plan that

00:17:06.929 --> 00:17:11.170
we then publish.
Malcolm Grant: Jo, there is quite a big challenge

00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:18.150
for us here, obviously. This is one of the
most wide-ranging, radical things that we

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:22.350
have done. You can envisage that, in some
of these areas, we are going to end up with

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:28.230
one or more, effectively, accountable care
organisations, which requires us to rethink

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:35.960
how we do the commissioning, and how we develop
the funding stream. How prepared are we to

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:40.370
bring in this thinking and develop this work
with those STPs?

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:47.730
Jo Lenaghan: Ian may want to say more on that.
So we also chair a working group, we work

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:52.630
with all the ALB policy leads. So what we
are doing now is we can get – the consequences

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:56.940
of the plans are issues that we need to look
at in terms of the enablers, the support,

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:01.120
what it means for commissioners, providers;
for the various guidancee that we put out over

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:06.140
the whole work stream, as we start to identify
exactly those issues. I do not know if Ian

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:08.800
wants to say anything?
Malcolm Grant: Ian, please.

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:15.540
Ian Dodge: I think there are two aspects to
that question. The first is to what extent

00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:24.400
the STPs as a planning coalition end up internalising
their allocation and agreeing a sum of money,

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:30.910
and that goes back to Noel’s question around
the pooling of sovereignty between different

00:18:30.910 --> 00:18:36.780
institutions, having a plan that works. You
obviously need that for the STPs.

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:42.920
There is then a second question. Within those
communities, to what extent to we need to

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:48.300
see new care models developing? You mentioned
accountable care organisations, whether PACS

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:57.780
or MCPs, which may be subsets of those STPs
which will be developed. Within the STP plans,

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:02.080
we have a large number of systems that are
very interested in developing those.

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:08.320
Malcolm Grant: I think the point is we have
to be ready with a support package, well thought

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:12.480
through, because the worst thing that could
happen is that we have launched an initiative

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:17.270
of which we are then obstructive, because
we have not got our own ducks in a row.

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:23.490
So I think, on behalf of the Board, we probably
felt quite wary and cautious about this when

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:28.030
we launched it, because it is so radical and
wide-reaching. What you have given us this

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:32.790
morning is really good assurance, and what
I think we are all listening to is responses

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:39.910
coming in from across England. When I met
with the chairs of the CCGs at my advisory

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:44.280
council, this was the one thing on which there
was total agreement: this was the only game

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:51.770
in town for them. The presence of local government
colleagues had been positive; challenging,

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:58.970
but actually very supportive. Thirdly, for
many of them there was a sort of dammed-up

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:03.580
sense of enthusiasm: they needed to do something
that they knew they wanted to do, but they

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:08.280
could not do. Hence my question about how
we help to change the structures to enable,

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:14.470
facilitate and promote the desirable benefits
that will come from this.

00:20:14.470 --> 00:20:19.910
I do not know whether there are other questions
or comments for Jo? No, there are not. Thank

00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:26.410
you very much. The Board will wish to follow
this very closely. This is a major opportunity

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:30.530
for us to change the way in which the NHS
provides services to patients.

00:20:30.530 --> 00:20:32.910
Jo Lenaghan: Thank you.
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

